WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.930
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:08.790
What a wonderful day to talk about fluid simulations!

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:14.301
This technique is an extension to Smoothed
Particle Hydrodynamics, or SPH in short, which

00:00:14.301 --> 00:00:19.300
is a widely used particle-based simulation
technique where the visual quality scales

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.689
with the number of simulated particles.

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:26.460
The more particles we use in the simulation,
the more eye candy we can expect.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.419
And the goal here is to create an extension
of these SPH-based simulations to include

00:00:31.419 --> 00:00:35.510
deformable bodies and granular materials to
the computations.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:40.890
This way, it is possible to create a scene
where we have instant coffee and soft candy

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.380
dissolving in water.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:47.320
Which not only looks beautiful, but sounds
like a great way to get your day started.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.770
Normally, we have to solve a separate set
of equations for each of the phases or material

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:58.570
types present, but because this proposed method
is able to put them in one unified equation,

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.820
it scales well with the number of materials
within the simulation.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.979
This is not only convenient from a theoretical
standpoint, but it also maps well to parallel

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:13.490
architectures and the results shown in the
video were run on a relatively high-end consumer

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.490
NVIDIA card.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:20.340
This is remarkable, as it should not be taken
for granted that a new fluid simulation technique

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.980
runs well on the GPU.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:27.119
The results indeed indicate that the number
of phases only have a mild effect on the execution

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:28.290
time of the algorithm.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:35.930
A nice and general framework for fluid-solid
interactions, dissolution, elastoplastic solids,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.700
and deformable bodies.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.540
What a fantastic value proposition.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.369
I could watch and play with these all day.

00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:47.110
I'll try my best to resist, but in case the
next episode is coming late, you know where

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:48.110
I am.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:52.520
The quality of the paper is absolutely top
tier and if you like physics, you're going

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.380
to have lots of fun reading it.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.039
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

